## 1. Introduction
Aqueous phosphate has drawn increasing attention in the past decades, due to its crucial role in the inducement of eutrophication or algal bloom in natural water. To deal with this problem, a plethora of methodologies of chemical treatment [1,2], biological method [3] and physical adsorption [4,5] have been successfully employed in phosphate contaminated water treatment. Nevertheless, the existing technologies are hardly to meet the increasing strict discharge levels, i.e., $0.01\mathrm{~mg~L^{-1}}$ in North USA [6] and $0.05~\mathrm{mg~L}^{-1}$ in EUR [7]. Along this line, the developing of novel and advanced materials are environmentally and ecologically essential to achieve efficient phosphate treatment.
In this respect, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been employed in environment remediation, due to their lager specific surface area (SSA) and abundant associated functional groups that are available for heavy metal/organic matter adsorption [8,9], and sufficient active sites that can realize catalytic degradation and chemical catalysis [10]. Despite of the acquired performance of bare MOFs as adsorbents, certain shortcoming of weaker adsorption affinity that are deriving from their vast void space and nonsaturated metallic centers can limit phosphate removal [11]. To address the contaminant removal, a larger amount of studies nowadays are paying close attention to the fabrication and optimization of diverse MOFs-based nanohybrids, for example, MOFs@engineering nanomaterials (MOFs@ENM) of MOFs@carbon nanotube (MOFs@CNT) and MOFs@graphene oxide $({\mathrm{MOFs@GO}}),$ Such-prepared MOFs@ENMs composites can significantly enhance contaminant removal performance compared to the bare MOFs by increasing the unsaturated adsorption sites and functional group [12-14]. In particular, Cao et al. successfully fabricate a threedimensional zeolitic imidazolate framework-8/graphene oxide (ZIF8/GO) composites that can significantly enhance the adsorption capacity of aqueous amphetamine to $61.97~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ [15]. Similarly, efficient Bisphenol-A removal of $97\%$ is achieved via a bimetallic MOFs@N-doped carbonaceous nanorods $(\mathsf{M O F s@N C})$ [16]. In addition to the efficient organic pollutant removal, satisfactory performance of MOFs-derivatives pertaining to aqueous toxic ions can be obtained. For example, Antonysamy J. and his cooperators have developed several carbonous material-modified MOFs, finding the optimum adsorption quantities of Fe@ABDC MOFs, Ce@ABDC, Zr@FuMOFs and $\mathtt{L a@F u}$ MOFs can respectively reach $4.92\:\:\mathrm{\mg}\:\:\mathrm{F}^{-1}\:\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $4.91\:\:\mathrm{\mg}\:\:\mathrm{F}^{-1}\:\:\mathrm{\bf~g}^{-1}$ , $4920~\mathrm{\mg}~\mathrm{F}^{-1}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ , and $4925\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{F}^{-1}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ [17-19]. However, notwithstanding the advances in contaminant removal, there still encounters daunting obstacles in separation and recycle for the exhausted adsorbents.
To this end, various technologies have been developed, Zhang et al. design a novel magnetic adsorbent harnessing a cis-diol structure towards boronate removal. Such-prepared adsorbents exhibit efficient boronate removal of $98\%$ and excellent adsorbent reutilization via magnetic iron in five cycles [20]. A $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -modified GO is employed as an adsorbent for the enrichment of rhodamine by Guo et al., finding the designed adsorbents not only harvest excellent removal efficiency of $99\%$ , but also exhibit good solid–liquid separation [21]. Likewise, Wang et al. prepare a flower-like nanohybrid adsorbent by coupling $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ with magnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ serving as a solid-phase extractant to facilitate the uptake of $\mathsf{H g}$ and Pb from contaminated water, simultaneously realize the easy-separation after usage. In addition to the higher contaminant removal efficiency that are benefited from magnetization modification, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ modification of adsorbents can clearly promote contaminant adsorption rate [22]. As such, the design of magnetic adsorbents are highly likely to possess the multi-advantages of excellent pollution removal, easy separation and rapid adsorption rate. Despite all these advances, there have been seldom studies systematically reporting the effect of associated functional groups of ENMs on the structure of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -modified hybrids, especially for the structure optimization pinpointing aqueous phosphate removal from real water.
Motivated by the aforementioned analysis on the current research gaps, we rationally designed a series of magnetic zeolitic imidazolate framework- $\cdot8@$ engineering nanomaterials (ZIF8@ENMs) architecture harnessing adsorptive ZIF8@ENMs and magnetic nanoparticles in target toward the efficient phosphate purification of phosphate-contaminated natural water. The presented work encompass four objectives: (i) to synthesize an emerging magnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -ZIF8@ENMs nanocomposite as a highly efficient adsorbent pinpointing phosphate removal; (ii) to assess the effect of different chemical conditions of pH, initial phosphate concentrations, coexisting ions and natural water on phosphate removal performance; (iii) to systematically probe the adsorption mechanisms of phosphate on such-designed $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}{-}Z\mathrm{IF}8@\mathrm{ENN}$ s hybrids; and (iv) to examine the reutilization and recycle of magnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}–\mathsf{Z I F}8@\$ ENMs nanohybrid in phosphate-contaminated water.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Adsorbent materials
ZIF8 and ENM. ZIF8 is synthesized based upon the methods reported in our previous study [23]. Pristine ENMs and ENMs harnessing amidogen, hydroxy and carboxy functional groups are bought from Beijing Boyu High-Tech Technology Co., Ltd.
ZIF8@ENM. Synthetic methodology of ZIF8 $@$ ENMs has been reported in the previous literature via 1, 2-dimethylimidazole and $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ of solvothermal method [24].
$F e_{3}{\cal O}_{4}–Z I F8@E N M$ . The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -ZIF8@ENMs composite is prepared by a coprecipitation method. In detail, $250~\mathrm{\mg}$ of MOF $@$ ENMs is added to $83.3~\mathrm{mL}$ of ultrapure water with a ultrasonic treatment for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . Afterwards, $_{0.301\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $\mathbf{0.173\g}$ of $\mathrm{FeSO_{4}}{\cdot}7\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ are added into the above prepared ZIF8 $@$ ENMs dispersion, respectively. Subsequently, adjust the pH to 10 by using $30\%\ (\mathrm{w/v})$ of ammonia, heating the solution to $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ with the addition of hydrazine hydrate $(4~\mathrm{mL})$ drop by drop. Meanwhile, stirring the mixture for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ and cooling the mixture to room temperature. Finally, drying off the composites after several wash of water and ethanol. 1, 2-dimethylimidazole, and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.$ - - $6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}{\cdot}7\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , ammonia, hydrazine hydrate are purchased from Beijing Honghu United Chemical Co. LTD. For the assessment of co-existing anion effect on phosphate, NaCl $(99.5\%)$ , $\Nu{{\bf a}_{2}}\tt C0_{3}(99.8\%),\tt N a N O_{3}(99.0\%)$ and $\Nu{42}\Im{0_{4}}\left(99.8\%\right)$ bought from Beijing Yili Fine Chemicals Co. LTD are used. All chemicals employed in this study require no further purification.
Natural surface water. Herein, two types of natural surface water are employed to probe phosphate-contaminated water properties on phosphate removal efficiency. A natural surface water (NW–1) is collected from Xiaotaihou River. Also, another natural surface water (NW–2) is sampled from a river named North Canal. To remove the particulate matters of aforementioned water samples, the collected two natural water are filtered through a binder-free, glass-fiber filter (Whatman, GF/C). Major characteristics of the two filtered natural water samples are presented in Table S1.
## 2.2. Characterization and analysis
Analysis. In the respect of single-system of phosphate, the phosphate concentration is tested by using the method of ammonium molybdate spectrophotometric method. The contents of aqueous phosphate species depending on different pH values are calculated by using Visual Minteq software, to probe pH effect on the existing form of phosphate. The binary-system of phosphate and co-existing ions/natural water is tested by the ion chromatograph (IC, Metrohm, Switzerland) harnessing a detection limit of $0.001~\mathrm{mg~L}^{-1}$ and a sample injection volume of $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ in this work to avoid the interference of ammonium molybdate spectrophotometric method.
## 2.3. Adsorption and regeneration experiments
Static adsorption experiment. For the feed water that merely contains phosphate, initial concentrations of 1, 2, 4, 6, 8 and $16\mathrm{~mg~L^{-1}~}$ harnessing a neutral pH condition of $\mathsf{p H}=7$ are employed to assess the maximum phosphate adsorption capacity at the temperature of $25~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The static adsorption process is completed by using a water bath thermostatic oscillator (SHZ-82, Yinggong Machinery co. LTD, China) at the speed of $200\ \mathrm{rpm\min^{-1}}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The effluent solution absorbance is measured by ammonium molybdate spectrophotometric method to analyze the phosphate removal efficiency. In the regard of the binary system of adsorption experiments, effect of co-existing anions of $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ , ${\mathsf{N O}}^{3-}$ , $S0_{4}^{2-}$ , and $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ harvesting a concentration of 1 mmol, on phosphate removal are probed. Phosphate removal efficiency of the optimized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ - ZIF8@ENM-OH in the binary system are assessed under different pH conditions of 5, 7 and 9 by using $0.1~\mathrm{\moL}$ of NaOH and 0.1 moL of HCl.
Dynamic adsorption experiment. To systematically assess the dynamic adsorption process and adsorption rate, dynamic adsorption experiments harnessing $200~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of phosphate (Conc. ${\bf\omega}=1~\mathrm{mg}$ ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ ) with an adsorbent dosage of $0.2\ \mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ is adopted. The dynamic adsorption experiments are completed under the condition of $200\mathrm{rpm}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathsf C$ Effluent samples are collected at the intervals of 5, 10, 15, 20, 30, 40, 60, 90, 120, 150, 180 min.
Regeneration experiment. The exhausted adsorbents are regenerated by employing $0.1\mathrm{~mol~L^{-1}}$ of NaOH solution, ultrapure water or $75\%$ of ethanol under $^\textrm{\scriptsize3h}$ of stirring [25-27], to compare the regeneration efficiency of different chemical solutions. Subsequently, to realize phosphate recycle, $\mathsf{C a}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ is added to the above desorbed phosphate-contaminated water to form $\mathsf{C a}_{3}\mathsf{P O}_{4}$ precipitation.
## 2.4. Computation
Calculation of phosphate removal efficiency. Phosphate removal percentages are calculated by using the equation (1) below:
$$
R_{P}=(1-\frac{C_{e}}{C_{o}})*100\%
$$
where $R_{P}\left(\%\right)$ is the removal percentage of phosphate, $C_{0}(\mathrm{mgL}^{-1})$ is phosphate concentration in the initial solution, while $\Sigma_{e}(\mathrm{mgL}^{-1})$ is equilibrium phosphate concentration in the effluent solution.
Adsorption isotherms fitting. The phosphate adsorption data for ZIF8@ENMs and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}–Z\mathrm{IF}8@\$ ENMs employed in this study are fitted to Freundlich model and Langmuir model, respectively. The Freundlich and optimized Langmuir adsorption equation is expressed as equation (2) and equation (3) [28]:
$$
Q_{e}=K C_{e}^{1/n}
$$
$$
Q_{e}=\frac{Q_{m}K C_{e}^{1/n}}{1+K C_{e}^{1/n}}
$$
where $C_{e}$ represents the equilibrium concentration of phosphate in effluent solution $(\mathrm{mgL^{-1}}\bar{.}$ ), $Q_{e}$ is behalf of the quantity of phosphate adsorbed per unit mass of adsorbents $\left(\mathrm{mg}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}\right)$ ), $K(\mathbf{g}\mathbf{\a}\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{\a}^{-1})$ is a constant related to the bonding energy between phosphate and the adsorbents, and $Q_{m}$ is the maximum adsorption capacity of contaminants $\left(\mathbf{mg\pg^{-1}}\right)$ ). The Langmuir model is designed to describe the multicomponent adsorption equilibrium.
Adsorption kinetics fitting. Dynamic adsorption models of pseudo first-order kinetic equation and pseudo second-order kinetic equation derived by Cai et al. are adopted here to simulate the breakthrough of aqueous contaminants through a porous adsorbent [29]. The pseudo first-order kinetic equation for dynamic phosphate adsorption can be described as equation (4):
$$
\ln\frac{q_{F}-q}{q_{F}}=-k_{1}t
$$
The pseudo second-order adsorption kinetics equation is as equation (5):
$$
\frac{1}{q_{\scriptscriptstyle F}-q}=\frac{1}{q_{\scriptscriptstyle F}}+k_{2}t
$$
where $q_{\mathrm{F}}$ and $q({\upmu}{\mathrm{g}}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1})$ represent the equilibrium concentrations of phosphate on various adsorbents at the filtration time of t, respectively; $k_{1}(\mathbf{h}^{-1})$ and $k_{2}$ $_2(\mathbf{g}\ \mathbf{m}\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{h}^{-1})$ are the pseudo first-order and pseudo second-order adsorption rate constants, respectively; and $t\left(\mathrm{{h}}\right)$ is the adsorption time.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Effect of adsorbent nanostructures on phosphate removal
Fig. 1a shows the synthetic procedure of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}}_{4}–\mathrm{ZIF}8@$ ENMs via a two-step of solvothermal reaction and coprecipitation method. To probe the effect of associated functional groups on ZIF8@ENMs nanohybrid structure and phosphate removal efficiency, pristine ENMs and ENMs harnessing $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , ACOOH and $-0\mathsf{H}$ are used to modify the bare ZIF8 (Fig. 1b). In detail, the respective average phosphate removal percentage of ZIF8@ENM, ZIF8@ENM- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , ZIF8 $@$ ENM-COOH and ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH are $60.50\%$ , $55.71\%$ , $77.74\%$ and $91.01\%$ , compared to $69.27\%$ of the bare ZIF8 and $0.85{-}3.05\%$ for the bare ENMs under the neutral condition (Fig. 1b). These results suggest the phosphate removal of ZIF8@ENM-COOH and ZIF8@ENM-OH heterostructure is superior to the other MOF@ENM hybrids. In this respect, the outstanding performance of ZIF8 $@$ ENM-COOH and ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH may be due to the successful chemical bonding formation between the oxygen-containing functional groups on ENMs and ZIF8 that can overcome the intrinsic disadvantages of non-saturated metallic centers that are available for coordination [23]. Compared to the phosphate removal percentage of $77.74\%$ by using ZIF8 $@$ ENM-COOH, the certain phosphate removal percentage increase to $91.01\%$ for ZIF8@ENM-OH. Such higher phosphate removal of ZIF8@ENM-OH may be due to the stronger chemical interaction formation between ZIF8 and $-0\mathsf{H}$ than that between ZIF8 and ACOOH. Upon the incorporation of ferroferric oxide, phosphate removal percentage further enhances to $91.72\%$ and $98.20\%$ for $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -ZIF8 $@$ ENM-COOH and $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}–Z I F8@E N M-O H}$ under the similar chemical conditions, respectively (Fig. 1c). In particular, phosphate removal percentage of ZIF8 $@$ ENM-COOH and ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH respectively enhances by $13.98\%$ and $7.19\%$ after the decoration of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ . The surface morphologies of pristine ENM-OH/ENM-COOH, ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH/ENMCOOH and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8@ENM-OH/ENM-COOH are examined in detail with the aid of SEM and TEM characterizations (Fig. 1d– 1i). Characteristic filamentous structures harnessing an average diameter of $4{\mathrm{-}}6\ \mathrm{\nm}$ are observed for the bare ENM-OH and ENM-COOH, respectively (Fig. 1d & 1 g). In respect to ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH and ZIF8 $@$ ENM-COOH, spherical particles possessing average diameters of $128~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and $164\mathrm{nm}$ are acquired, respectively, implying smaller ZIF8 particles are formed on ENM-OH than that on ENM-COOH (Fig. 1e & 1 h). In this respect, the morphology of acquired spherical particles are exactly similar to the formed ZIF8 particles reported in other literature [24]. After the magnetic treatment, a characteristic hybrid is formed by spherical ZIF8 particles, tubular ENMs and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles. At the same time, a layered double structure is achieved on the tubular ENMs dotted with spherical ZIF8 particles (Fig. 1a, Fig. 1f & 1i). Indeed, the average particle size of ZIF8 on ENM-COOH is larger than that on ENMOH (164 vs. $128~\mathrm{nm}$ ), suggesting ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH can provide more available sites for the growth of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . Along this line, the higher phosphate removal efficiency of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ -ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH than that of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8 $@$ ENM-COOH are benefiting from the larger content of ZIF8 and $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles.
## 3.2. Characterization and property of the phosphate-selective MOF@ENMs heterostructures
To probe the successful incorporation and chemical compositions/properties of the phosphate-selective MOF@ENMs heterostructures, XRD and XPS analysis are investigated (Fig. 2a– 2b). Fig. 2a shows the XRD analysis of ENM-OH, ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -ZIF8@ENM-OH. In particular, ENM-OH displays the characteristic peaks at $25.4^{\circ}$ (224) and $43.6^{\circ}$ (1 0 1). In contrast, the diffraction peaks of ZIF8 correspond well with $7.27^{\circ}$ (0 11), $10.2^{\circ}$ (00 2), $12.6^{\circ}$ (1 1 2), $14.6^{\circ}$ (0 2 2), $16.3^{\circ}$ (0 13), $17.8^{\circ}$ (2 2 2), $19.4^{\circ}$ (1 23), $21.9^{\circ}$ (11 4), $24.3^{\circ}$ (2 2 3), $25.4^{\circ}$ (2 2 4), $26.4^{\circ}$ (0 1 5), $29.4^{\circ}$ (044), suggesting the successful formation of ZIF8 on $Z\mathrm{IF}8@$ ENM. Also, the characteristic peaks at $30.0^{\circ}$ (220), $35.4^{\circ}$ (311), $43.0^{\circ}$ (400), $53.7^{\circ}$ (422), $57.2^{\circ}$ (511) and $62.70^{\circ}$ (440) are corresponding to $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ [30]. The XRD diffraction results of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH show that the ZIF8 particle surface are primarily covered by $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ , resulting in weak ZIF8 spectrum signals on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH, which are consistent with SEM and
TEM results (Fig. 1f). Pertaining to XPS characterization, survey spectra verify the existence of C, N, O and Zn elements on the ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH nanohybrids, while C, N, O, Zn and Fe elements on the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH composites (Fig. 2b). In the highresolution of C 1s spectrum (Fig. 2h), three peaks at $283.93\ \mathrm{eV}$ 284.90 eV and 285.70 eV are assigned to ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{=}}{\mathsf{C}}$ , CAN and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ which possess the respective content of $76\%$ , $14\%$ , and $10\%$ (Table S2). After the modification of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ , the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ part of the adsorbents disappears, while the ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{=}}{\mathsf{C}}$ percentage increases to $47\%$ and the $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ content decreases to $53\%$ . Meanwhile, little variation of $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}H}$ singles at the peak of $399.01\ \mathrm{eV}$ is observed in the N 1 s spectrum, suggesting the stable ZIF8 content on $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}.$ modified ZIF8@ENM-OH. Considering the high-resolution of the XPS O 1 s analysis, $Z\mathrm{n}{-}0$ peak position shifts from $531.14~\mathrm{eV}$ to $530.83\mathrm{eV}$ , while a new peak of FeAO with a content of $30\%$ appears at 529.51 eV (Fig. 2e & Table S2). In this case, the Fe $2{\tt p}3/2$ and Fe $2{\tt p}1/2$ corresponding to the peaks of Fe in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ at $710.75\ \mathrm{eV}$ and 724.79 eV, and the O 1 s characteristic peaks of 529.51 eV verify the FeAO bonding formation between $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ and CNT/ZIF8 (Fig. 2b & 2e). Little variation of the $Z\mathfrak{n}\ 2\mathfrak{p}$ spectrum in highresolution between ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8@ENM-OH indicate the steady ZIF8 content after magnetic modification (Fig. S1).
## 3.3. Ease-of-separation performance of $F e_{3}O_{4}$ -MOF@ENM
In addition to the obviously enhanced phosphate removal of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -ZIF8@ENM, good ease-of-separation performance is acquired. Fig. 2g shows the schematic diagram of the solid–liquid separation performance of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8 $@$ ENMs adsorbents. The separation of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -decorated adsorbent could be achieved in a instantaneous time of 10 s (Fig. 2h inset). In this regard, vibrating sample magnetometer (VSM) of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}–\mathrm{ZIF}8@\mathrm{E}$ NMs is tested to verify the magnetization. Fig. 2h shows that $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}–Z I F8@E I}$ NMs nanocomposites manifest a favorable superparamagnetic property harnessing a saturation magnetization value of 55.07 emu $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $5000~\mathrm{K},$ which is smaller than the saturation magnetization value of 80.00 emu $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $5000~\mathrm{K}$ for the bare $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ (Fig. 2h). Such acquired saturation magnetization of the prepared $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ - ZIF8@ENMs is much higher than the similar $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -decorated adsorbents [31]. It is evident for the faster adsorbent collection from water than the similar adsorbents (i.e. 30 s, 120 s) [32], demonstrating the excellent ease-of-separation efficiency towards practical application. Such good magnetic properties can be attributed to the high content of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ . To clarify the chemical composition content of adsorbents, the TGA of pure $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}.$ ZIF8@ENM-OH nanohybrids have been presented in Fig. 2i. The acquired TGA curve of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}–Z I F8@E N M-O H}$ can be divided into four stages. In detail, the first weight loss stage of $2.07\%$ can be ascribed to the adsorbed water in the sample. Secondly, the weight loss occurs at $106{\scriptstyle-439}^{\circ}{\textsf{C}}$ can be attributed to the decomposition of associated functional groups on nanohybrids. Subsequently, some remnant carbon on the sorbents decompose continuously within the temperature of $440{\mathrm{-}}650{\mathrm{~\textdegreeC}}.$ . At the last stage of above $650~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ no significant weight loss is observed, implying the $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ content is $89.20\%$ . In this respect, the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8 $@$ ENM
OH can be calculated as $86.23\%$ which ensures the quick separation of adsorbents via such strong magnetism and clarifies the hybrid chemical compositions.
## 3.4. Adsorption isotherms of phosphate on MOF@ENMs nanohybrids
Adsorption isotherms of pristine ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ - ZIF8@ENM-OH towards phosphate are shown in Fig. 3a and Table 2. The experimental fitting data of Langmuir model and Freundlich model is similar due to the same ${\tt R}^{2}$ values of 0.980–0.997. Considering the heterogeneous adsorption of the prepared adsorbents, the Langmuir model is more suitable to interpret the experimental data in the following sections. In this respect, the maximum phosphate adsorption capacity of ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8@ENMOH is $32.8~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $441.7\ \mathrm{mg\g^{-1}}$ under the neutral condition according to Langmuir fitting results. Collectively, the acquired results indicate the maximum phosphate adsorption capacity $(q_{m})$ of magnetic ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH is about 13.5 times higher than the pristine ones. Also, this outstanding performance pertaining to phosphate surpass most of the state-of-the-art adsorbents in current studies (Fig. 3b&Table 3), which may be due to the strong chemisorption that facilitate adsorption affinity.
## 3.5. Dynamic adsorption of phosphate on adsorbents
## 3.6. pH effect on phosphate removal
pH value plays a significant role during phosphate adsorption process. As such, various initial pH values of $\mathsf{p H}=5$ , $\mathsf{p H}=7$ and $\mathsf{p H}=\mathsf{g}$ representing acidic, neutral and alkaline conditions are employed, to investigate the pH influence on phosphate adsorption performance by using MOF $@$ ENMs and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}–\mathrm{MOF@EN}$ Ms (Fig. 1c). In detail, the phosphate removal efficiency of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ - ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8 $@$ ENM-COOH is $96.8\%$ and $87.6\%$ at $\mathsf{p H}=5$ , respectively. When the initial pH is adjusted to $\mathsf{p H}=9$ , the phosphate removal efficiencies of the two nanohybrids are $94.6\%$ and $82.3\%$ , respectively. Comparatively analyzed, the removal efficiency of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}–Z I F8@E N M-O H}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8 $@$ ENM-COOH at $\mathsf{p H}=7$ is superior to that under the acidic and alkaline conditions. Similar excellent performance pertaining to phosphate removal under neutral condition have been reported in other studies [49]. Thus-derived results can guide aqueous phosphate treatment in practical application, i.e. the pH of the targeted phosphatecontaminated water can be simply and economically adjusted to neutral to optimize treatment technology and enhance phosphate removal efficiency.
## 3.7. Simultaneous adsorption of phosphate and co-existing anions
Co-existing anions might manifest crucial impact on phosphate adsorption performance via mutual competition, due to the complexity of practical water. Along this line, considering the similar properties of phosphate and anions, only anions of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ , $S0_{4}^{2-}$ , and $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ are employed to analysis the co-existing anion effect on phosphate removal efficiency. As shown in Fig. 5, the presence of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ , $S0_{4}^{2-}$ , and $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ do not exhibit markable influence on phosphate adsorption by using $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}–Z\mathrm{IF}8@\mathrm{ENM}$ nanohybrids. In detail, the removal efficiencies of phosphate with coexisting $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ are $97\%$ , $98\%$ , $94\%$ at $\mathsf{p H}=5$ , $\mathsf{p H}=7$ and $\mathsf{p H}=\mathsf{9}$ , respectively. In presence of ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ , the removal percentages of phosphate are $98\%$ at $\mathsf{p H}=5$ , $98\%$ at $\mathrm{\pH}=7$ , and $95\%$ at $\mathsf{p H}=\mathsf{9}$ . Similarly, the respective phosphate removal percentage are $97\%$ , $98\%$ and $93\%$ at $\mathsf{p H}=5$ , $\mathsf{p H}=7$ and $\mathsf{p H}=\mathsf{9}$ within $S0_{4}^{2-}$ . Collectively, the presence of $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ , ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ , $S0_{4}^{2-}$ has no negative effect on phosphate removal. In contrast, slight decline $(3.63\substack{-4.20\%})$ in phosphate removal have been observed in presence of $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ in the binary system, which may be attributed to the competition adsorption between $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ and phosphate [50,51]. Such-acquired results indicate that the presence of anions can hardly hinder the phosphate adsorption onto $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}–Z\mathrm{IF}8@\mathrm{E}$ NM nanohybrids, implying its potential application in complex real water. Thus, the obtained results fully indicate that the magnetic ZIF8-modified ENMs adsorbent has high selectivity pertaining to phosphate, excellent antifouling property within complex anions, enable it a promising application prospect.
## 3.8. Regeneration of the nanostructured adsorbents
Efficient regeneration methods are capable of extending the life of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8@ENM-OH adopted as the adsorbents for phosphate treatment, to save the adsorbents’ cost. Herein, the prepared $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8@ENM-OH is born to harvest the advantages of easycollection by a magnet. Such exhausted $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -ZIF8@ENM-OH is subsequently regenerated by using $0.1\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ of NaOH, ultrapure water and $75\%$ of ethanol, respectively. By using $0.1\mathrm{\mol\L^{-1}}$ of NaOH as the regeneration solution, the phosphate removal percentage slightly decrease to $90\%$ in the second cycle compared to the first cycle of $98\%$ . Afterwards, the phosphate removal percentage remains stable at about $81\%$ in the following consecutive cycles (Fig. 6a), suggesting the phosphate removal percentage can be above $80\%$ even in multi-regeneration cycles. In comparison, both of the phosphate removal percentages gradually decrease with the ever-increasing regeneration cycles of ultrapure water and $75\%$ of ethanol (Fig. 6b & Fig. 6c), resulting in $70\%$ and $41\%$ of phosphate removal in the fifth cycle, respectively. Thus, the regeneration of NaOH is highly likely to harvest the optimum efficiency pertaining to phosphate removal percentage in consecutive regeneration cycles. Furthermore, $\mathsf{C a}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ is added to the aforementioned desorbed phosphate-contaminated water to form $\mathsf{C a}_{3}\mathsf{P O}_{4}$ precipitation to realize phosphate reutilization. Along this line, the high phosphate-selective adsorbents of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}–Z I F8@E N M-O H}$ are highly likely to possess ease-of-separation and good regeneration efficiency to implement the recycle of the adsorbents and phosphate.
## 3.9. Application in realistic phosphate-contaminated practical natural water
Two natural surface water is employed in this study to investigate the application of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8 $@$ ENMs in practical water purification. Table S1 summaries the chemical property of two typical natural water. In this respect, the initial phosphate concentrations of NW–1 and NW–2 are $1.42~\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ and $1.07\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ , respectively. Fig. 7a shows the phosphate removal efficiencies are $0\%$ for the pristine ENM-OH and ENM-COOH both in NW–1 and NW–2. After the modification of ZIF8, the phosphate adsorption respectively increases to $45.72\%$ for ZIF8@ENM-OH and $43.15\%$ for ZIF8@ENM-COOH in NW–1. Consistently, the removal percentage of phosphate increases to $70.63\%$ and $63.05\%$ for ZIF8@ENM-OH and ZIF8 $@$ ENM-COOH in NW–2, respectively. When the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ is incorporated into the ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH composites, phosphate removal percentages further enhance to $89.50\%$ and $98.00\%$ in NW–1 and NW–2, respectively. Accordingly, phosphate removal percentage of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -ZIF8 $@$ ENM-COOH is $86.22\%$ in NW–1 and $90.50\%$ in NW–2. Thus, different decrease extents in phosphate removal percentage in natural water are observed by using pristine ZIF8@ENMs and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}{-}Z\mathrm{IF}8@\mathrm{EN}1$ M. In particular, compared with the synthetic water of $1\mathrm{\mg\L}^{-1}$ , phosphate removal percentage of ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH and ZIF8 $@$ ENM-COOH respectively decreases by $20.38\%$ and $14.69\%$ in NW–2 with the similar initial phosphate concentrations. In comparison, the decrease of phosphate removal by using $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -ZIF8@ENM-COOH is $0.20\%$ and $1.22\%$ , respectively. Thus, the decline in phosphate removal of ZIF8@ENMs is much larger than that of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}.$ - ZIF8@ENM, implying the stronger antifouling property of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}.$ ZIF8@ENMs than the pristine ZIF8 $@$ ENMs adsorbents in the real water treatment.
In addition to the outstanding performance of phosphate removal, excellent natural organic matter removal is acquired for $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -decorated adsorbents. Five fluorescence peaks are identified with the two natural surface waters of NW–1 and NW–2, according to their excitation/emission wavelength regions (Fig. 7c & 7d). In general, such substances are categorized as aromatic proteins (regions I and II), fulvic acid-like materials (region III), soluble microbial byproduct-like material (region IV), and humic acid-like organics (region V) [52]. As for NW–1, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -MOF $@$ ENMs adsorption reduces the intensities of peak I, II, III, IV and $\mathsf{v}$ by $44.2\%$ , $30.7\%$ , $93.3\%$ , $80.1\%$ and $75.1\%$ . In the case of NW–2, the peak intensities decrease by $37.7\%$ , $18.1\%$ , $83.7\%$ , $79.3\%$ and $73.4\%$ , respectively (Fig. 7 vs. Table S4). As a result, major of the organic substances are simultaneously removed in combination with aqueous phosphate, suggesting the promising application of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\ensuremath{0_{4}}}.$ - MOF@ENMs in practical water treatment.
## 3.10. Theoretical insight of phosphate adsorption on $F e_{3}O_{4}$ - MOF@ENMs
To gain fundamental insight into the phosphate adsorption mechanisms onto $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -decorated ZIF8@ENMs adsorption process, adsorbent characterization, experimental adsorption results in combination with theoretical calculation are systematically analyzed. In this regard, electrostatic attraction, ligand exchange, complexation interaction, lewis acid-base interaction, hydrogen bond interaction and electrostatic repulsion are considered based upon the relating previous study [53]. Slightly declined contents of $Z\mathrm{n}$ $0.47\%$ vs. $0.39\%$ , Fe $(3.74\%$ vs. $3.09\%$ ) and C ( $76.36\%$ vs. $85.20\%$ , and increased contents of O $6.59\%$ vs. $9.6\%$ ) and $\mathrm{~\sf~P~}$ ( $0.00\%$ vs. $0.41\%$ are observed (Fig. 8a). The increased O and $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ content implies the successful phosphate adsorption onto the adsorbents. High-resolution view of C 1 s spectrum depicts that ${\mathsf{C}}{=}{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}{-}{\mathsf{C}}$ ,
$C-N$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ contents are $74\%$ , $10\%$ and $16\%$ for the used ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH, respectively (Fig. 8b). Similarly, the ${\mathsf{C}}{=}{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}{-}{\mathsf{C}}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ contents are respectively $80\%$ and $20\%$ for the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}.$ decorated ZIF8@ENM-OH. These results declare that the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ content improves both for the pristine and phosphate adsorbed $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}.$ - decorated ZIF8@ENM, which may be closely related to the formation of $\mathtt{P-O}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=}0$ contents on the adsorbents [54]. As for the high-resolution XPS spectrum of O 1 s (Fig. 8c), $Z\mathrm{n}{-}0$ content decreases, while $Z\mathrm{n-O-P}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=}0$ percentages obviously increase for the used ZIF8@ENM, suggesting the $Z\mathrm{n}{-}0{-}\mathrm{P}$ interaction formation during phosphate adsorption onto ZIF8@ENMs nanohybrids. Previous study has demonstrated the dominated interactions of $Z\mathrm{n-O-P}$ and H-bond during phosphate adsorption onto $Z\mathrm{IF}8@$ ENMs composites, according to the analysis of experimental results and density functional theory (DFT) simulation [23]. Further analysis of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}\mathrm{ZIF}8@\mathrm{ENM-OH}$ after phosphate adsorption reveals that the peak position of $Z\mathrm{n}{-}0$ shifts from $530.83~\mathrm{eV}$ to 531 eV. In this respect, the FeAO, $Z\mathrm{n}{-}0$ , FeAOAP, $Z\mathrm{n-O-P}/\protect$ $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=}0$ and $0{-}\mathsf{C}{=}0$ percentages of the used $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8@ENM-OH are $49\%$ , $16\%$ , $7\%$ and $2\%$ , respectively. In comparison with the bare $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}-Z I F8@E N M-O H},$ , the obviously increased percentage of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Fe-O-P}$ explains the fact of the enhanced phosphate removal after the incorporation of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ into ZIF8@ENM-OH nanohybrids. In addition, comparing the Fe $2{\tt p}$ spectra of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH before and after adsorption (Fig. 2f vs. Fig. 8e), the binding energy of Fe 2p peak shift lower, may due to a transfer of electrons while forming FeAOAP bond (Table S2) [55].
Pertaining to the FTIR spectrum of pristine $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}–Z I F8@E l}$ NMOH and $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{Z I F}8@\mathsf{E N M-O H}$ after phosphate adsorption are displayed in Fig. S2. In regard to the original $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{\overline{{{Z}}I F8@E N M-O H}}$ strong bands around absorption peaks at $545~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are observed, which are corresponding to FeAO vibration from the magnetite phase. Also, the peaks of $1600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $3640~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ respectively represent $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ and $-0\mathsf{H}$ groups. After phosphate adsorption, FeAO, $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ andAOH absorption peaks remain stable, whilst a new peak at $1006~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ relating to adsorbed phosphate appears. Such peak for phosphate adsorption can be observed at the similar position in other literatures [56,57]. These results indicate that the magnetic materials have successfully adsorbed aqueous phosphate with a relatively stable structure. Therefore, the associated functional groups of used $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{ZIF}8@\mathrm{ENM-OH}$ remain stable in comparison with the pristine one based on FTIR results. To probe the chemical composition variation, TGA of phosphate adsorbed $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{\overline{{{Z}}I F8@E N M-O H}}$ is conducted. In this respect, the weight loss of first stage is $3.40\%$ . Secondly, $5.21\%$ of weight loss pertaining to the decomposition of associated functional groups on nanohybrids occurs. After the last two stages of weight loss, $87.43\%$ of weight $\left(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\right)$ still remains, implying the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ content of the used adsorbent is $84.52\%$ . Consequently, compared with the pristine adsorbents, there is no significant variation in the chemical compositions of phosphate adsorbed $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}\mathrm{ZIF}8@\mathrm{ENM-OH}$ . These findings of XPS, FTIR and TGA indicate that the magnetic materials have successfully adsorbed aqueous phosphate with a relatively stable structure. Based upon the results of BET, the SSA of pristine $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8@ENM-OH and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}–\mathrm{ZIF}8@\mathrm{ENM-OH}$ after phosphate adsorption is 192.30 and $160.15\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Table 1). The small decline in SSA of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}\mathrm{ZIF}8@\mathrm{ENM-OH}$ after phosphate adsorption may be derived from the formation of more compact porous structure after aqueous phosphate adsorption. Despite of the decreased SSA results of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -ZIF8@ENM-OH after phosphate adsorption, there is no obvious variation of SEM morphology in Fig. 1f and Fig. S3, verifying the stability of prepared adsorbents in our study. Collectively, the inherent chemical and physical stability enable the long-term application of prepared adsorbents in our study.
In addition to the aforementioned interactions of $Z\mathrm{n-O-P}$ , ${\mathsf{F e}}{\mathrm{-}}0{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{P}}$ and H-bond, electrostatic interaction plays a crucial role in aqueous phosphate removal Fig. 9. Fig. 8h shows the isoelectric points of the ZIF8 $@$ ENM-OH and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -ZIF8@ENM-OH are respectively at $\mathsf{p H}=2.5$ and $\mathsf{p H}=4.0$ , implying the employed adsorbents are negatively charged at $\mathsf{p H}=5\mathsf{-9}$ . Accordingly, phosphate exists as $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{-}$ $(99.22\%)$ and $\mathsf{H P O}_{4}^{2-}$ $(0.63\%)$ at $\mathsf{p H}=5$ , $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{-}$ $(60.92\%)$ 1 and $\mathrm{HPO}_{4}^{2-}$ $(30.07\%)$ at $\mathrm{pH}=7$ and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{-}$ $(1.52\%)$ and $\mathsf{H P O}_{4}^{2-}$ $(98.43\%)$ at $\mathsf{p H}=\mathsf{9}$ (Fig. 8f). Along this line, the electrostatic repulsion between the negatively charged phosphate and the more negatively charged adsorbents is larger at $\mathsf{p H}=\mathsf{9}$ than that at $\mathsf{p H}=5$ , resulting in a higher phosphate removal at $\mathsf{p H}=5$ than that at $\mathsf{p H}=\mathsf{9}$ . In addition, due to the release of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ under acidic conditions, the adsorption removal efficiency at $\mathsf{p H}=5$ can be lower than that at $\mathsf{p H}=7$ . These results indicate that the electrostatic attraction between the adsorbent and phosphate is negligible, implying that the removal of phosphate by $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}–Z I F8@E N M-O H}$ is chemisorption (including ligand exchange of $Z\mathrm{n}{-}0{-}\mathrm{P}$ , complexation of FeAOAP and H-bond) rather than electrostatic attraction [58].
## 4. Conclusion
Herein, a novel MOF $@$ ENM nanohybrid pinpointing phosphate removal has been designed via a two-step hydrothermal and coprecipitation methodology by growing ZIF8 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ on various functional ENMs. Amongst the prepared adsorbents of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ - MOF@ENMs, ENMs containing hydroxyl and carboxyl groups are highly likely to form stronger chemical bond between ENMs and ZIF8, facilitating phosphate adsorption. After the modification of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ , the maximum phosphate adsorption capacity of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}.$ - MOF@ENM-OH can reach $441.7\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is 13.5 times higher than the bare MOF@ENM-OH and evidently surpasses the state-ofthe-art adsorbents [36,39]. Various chemical conditions of pH values and co-existing anions are systematically assessed. In detail, the neutral pH condition is highly likely to promote phosphate removal compares to acidic ${\mathrm{[pH}}=5{\mathrm{]}}$ ) and alkline $\mathrm{\boldmath~\left.~\pH_{\Sigma}\right.}=9\mathrm{\boldmath~\cdot~}$ conditions. No adverse impact on phosphate removal is observed in presence of $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ , ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ and $S0_{4}^{2-}$ , and high phosphate removal efficiency of $591\%$ remains for co-existing $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ , enables the practical applications of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -MOF $@$ ENM-OH in complex natural water. Chemisorption of $Z\mathrm{n}{-}0{-}\mathrm{P}$ , FeAOAP and electrostatic interactions is demonstrated to be the primary mechanisms for the uptake of phosphate, by the analysis of experimental adsorption results in combination with characterizations. Such-obtained adsorption mechanisms are consistent with the conclusions of similar magnetic adsorbents in Li’s group and Zhai’s group [59,60].
In addition to the obviously enhanced phosphate removal efficiency, the magnetic MOF@ENM hybrids exhibit easy-ofseparation within $10~s$ , ensuring the efficient adsorbent collection and recycle. Such-acquired outstanding performance of phosphate removal and easy-of-separation remain stable in five consecutive adsorption-regeneration recycles, demonstrating the prepared magnetic MOF@ENMs are promising recyclable sorbents and potential application on phosphate-contaminated water treatment.
